# C Database Engine

A relational database engine built from scratch in C for Linux[cite: 1]. It features a file-backed page storage layer and an in-memory buffer pool manager that writes and persists fixed 4096-byte blocks directly to disk[cite: 1]. The engine incorporates B-tree indexing for logarithmic primary key lookups, directly mapping keys to physical page and offset coordinates[cite: 1]. It also includes a custom SQL execution engine and parser supporting core query operations like `INSERT`, full-scan `SELECT`, and index-filtered `WHERE` queries[cite: 1].

## Architectural Overview

The engine separates concerns into three distinct layers: storage, indexing, and query execution.

*   **Disk-Backed Pager (`pager.c`)**: Manages persistent file storage using fixed 4096-byte pages via raw POSIX file descriptors, allocating new pages dynamically as record capacity expands.
*   **Buffer Pool Manager (`pager.c`)**: Caches active page pointers in memory for sub-microsecond latency and intercepts shutdown signals to flush all dirty pages safely to disk.
*   **B-Tree Index (`btree.c`)**: Implements a balanced multi-way search tree to eliminate sequential scanning for targeted lookups (O(log n) complexity), mapping internal keys directly to physical `(page_index, row_offset)` coordinates.
*   **SQL Execution Engine (`main.c`)**: A continuous Read-Eval-Print Loop (REPL) that parses commands, validates schema bounds, and routes queries to either the sequential scanner or the B-tree index.

## On-Disk Storage Layout

Data files (`database.db`) are laid out as contiguous 4096-byte binary chunks:

    +-------------------------------------------------------------+
    |                          Page 0                             |
    |  [num_rows: uint32]  [Row 0]  [Row 1]  [Row 2] ... [Row 31] |
    +-------------------------------------------------------------+
    |                          Page 1                             |
    |  [num_rows: uint32]  [Row 0]  [Row 1]  [Row 2] ... [Row 31] |
    +-------------------------------------------------------------+

Each record is packed into a fixed-width 68-byte binary layout:
*   `id` (uint32_t): 4 bytes
*   `username` (char[32]): 32 bytes
*   `email` (char[32]): 32 bytes

## Getting Started

### Prerequisites
*   GCC or Clang compiler
*   GNU Make
*   POSIX-compliant operating system (Linux, macOS)

### Build and Run
Clone the repository and compile using the provided Makefile:

    git clone https://github.com/MustafaMaj23/c-db-engine.git
    cd c-db-engine
    make
    ./db_engine

## Supported Commands

| Command | Syntax | Description |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Insert** | `INSERT <id> <username> <email>` | Writes to the lowest available disk page slot and pushes the new key to the B-Tree. |
| **Scan** | `SELECT` | Performs a sequential physical scan across every allocated disk page and active row offset. |
| **Lookup** | `SELECT WHERE id = <id>` | Bypasses sequential scanning by querying the B-tree for the exact coordinate. |
| **Quit** | `EXIT` | Flushes all in-memory buffers to disk and exits cleanly. |

## Example Session

    Custom C Database Engine Initialized. Type 'EXIT' to quit.
    db > INSERT 1 alice alice@domain.com
    Inserted row (id: 1) at page 0, offset 0.
    db > INSERT 2 bob bob@domain.com
    Inserted row (id: 2) at page 0, offset 1.
    db > SELECT
    (1, alice, alice@domain.com)
    (2, bob, bob@domain.com)
    db > SELECT WHERE id = 1
    Found: ID=1, Username=alice, Email=alice@domain.com
    db > EXIT

